    {
      "id": "gita-9-4-5",
      "locus": "Bhagavad-Gītā Bhāṣya 9.4–5",
      "cite": "sankara/gita-bhasya/9.4-5",
      "source_label": "BhGBh 9.4–5",
      "iast": "mayā tatam idaṃ sarvaṃ jagad avyakta-mūrtinā\nmat-sthāni sarva-bhūtāni na cāhaṃ teṣv avasthitaḥ\nna ca mat-sthāni bhūtāni paśya me yogam aiśvaram\nbhūta-bhṛn na ca bhūta-stho mamātmā bhūta-bhāvanaḥ",
      "english": "All this world is pervaded by me in my unmanifest form. All beings are situated in me, yet I am not situated in them. And yet beings are not situated in me: behold my divine yoga. I bear beings but am not established in them; my Self is the sustainer and generator of beings.",
      "grammar": "mayā is instrumental, the agent of pervasion. tatam is the past passive participle 'pervaded'. idaṃ sarvaṃ jagat is the full extent of the pervaded world. avyakta-mūrtinā qualifies the mode of presence. mat-sthāni uses a locative relation, 'situated in me'. teṣu and mat-sthāni are reciprocally opposed in the two verses. bhūta-bhṛt, bhūta-sthaḥ, and bhūta-bhāvanaḥ are nominal epithets that preserve non-involvement while asserting sustaining power.",
      "words": [
        { "i": 0, "iast": "mayā", "gloss": "by me", "morph": "instr. sg. pronoun" },
